{"schema_version":1,"task_id":"theory-3d05f1129fa8cea0","paper_id":"2510.25785v1","variant":"original","kind":"theory","outcome":"checked","reason":null,"error":null,"findings":[{"finding_id":"theory-3d05f1129fa8cea0-f0","task_id":"theory-3d05f1129fa8cea0","paper_id":"2510.25785v1","variant":"original","error_class":"math_error","anchor":{"kind":"sentence","sentence_id":"s161","start":159,"end":206},"target":{"kind":"setting","key":"math"},"summary":"Section 5.4: The text states that a latency of 0.99 ms per sample (or ~1,010 samples/s) is equivalent to processing '≈2.8 hours of signal per minute of wall time'. However, given that each sample represents 10 seconds of signal, 1,010 samples/s corresponds to 10,100 seconds of signal per second of wall time, which equals approximately 2.8 hours of signal per second (or about 168 hours per minute) ","explanation":"high confidence. The text states that a latency of 0.99 ms per sample (or ~1,010 samples/s) is equivalent to processing '≈2.8 hours of signal per minute of wall time'. However, given that each sample represents 10 seconds of signal, 1,010 samples/s corresponds to 10,100 seconds of signal per second of wall time, which equals approximately 2.8 hours of signal per second (or about 168 hours per minute) of wall time, making the stated rate off by a factor of 60. Witness: 2.8 hours of signal per second","evidence":[{"kind":"quote","source":{"kind":"submission","id":"2510.25785v1","title":null,"url":null},"text":"or ≈2.8 hours of signal per minute of wall time","page":null,"char_start":159,"char_end":206,"path":null,"line_start":null,"line_end":null}],"uncertain":false}],"attempts":1}
